.TH SDR 1
.SH NAME
sdr/fm, sdr/am, sdr/rawiq, sdr/iqhdr \- demodulate a software-defined radio's samples
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sdr/fm
[
.B -nwR
] [
.B -c
.I chans
] [
.B -d
.I µs
] [
.B -g
.I gain
] [
.B -o
.I rate
]
.PP
.B sdr/am
[
.B -R
] [
.B -b
.I hertz
] [
.B -c
.I chans
] [
.B -g
.I gain
] [
.B -o
.I rate
]
.PP
.B sdr/rawiq
.B -r
.I rate
[
.B -f
.I freq
]
.PP
.B sdr/iqhdr
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Fm
and
.I am
each read a radio's samples on their standard input, as
.IR sdrfs (4)'s
.B iq
gives them: a header, then 8-bit unsigned I then Q.
The header is in
.IR audio (6)'s
form, lines that are each a command
.IR sdrfs 's
.B ctl
takes, an empty line, the whole a multiple of 4 bytes:
.IP
.EX
rate 2400000
enc cu8
freq 99500000
.EE
.PP
The rate in is the header's; input with no header, or with an
.B enc
other than
.BR cu8 ,
is refused.
They write the sound the samples carry on their standard output, in
.IR audio (6)'s
form too: a header
.RB ( rate ,
.BR chans ,
.BR "bits 16" ,
.BR "enc pcm" ),
so that
.IR auplay (1)
sets
.B audioctl
from it, then
.IR audio (3)'s
16-bit little-endian samples,
.I chans
of each, the same sound in every channel.
With
.B -R
the sound is raw, with no header, and
.B audioctl
is the caller's to set.
A format is said once, by the stream that has it: nothing is told a
rate twice.
The radio is tuned to the station; what is a little off (a frequency
error, an offset in ppm) is heard all the same.
They are filters: they read until end of file and keep no state
elsewhere.
.I Sdrfs
ends a stream when it is retuned, so a demodulator ends with it; run it
again on
.B iq
for the new frequency.
.PP
.I Fm
demodulates FM: wide, broadcast FM, by default (or
.BR -w ),
and narrow with
.B -n
(marine VHF, 2 m amateur, NOAA weather, the land mobile bands).
The samples are summed to about 240k a second; narrow, the channel
(8 kHz either side) is filtered out of them and they are decimated to
about 48k.
The angle each turns from the last is the sound.
Wide, it is de-emphasised,
.B -d
microseconds: 50, as broadcast in most of the world, 75 in the Americas,
0 none (narrow has none unless asked).
Wide, the sound is kept below 15 kHz (above are the stereo pilot and
subcarriers, and RDS), and below 0.4 of the rate out; it is mono.
Full deviation (75 kHz wide, 5 kHz narrow) is 0.7 of full scale.
.PP
.I Am
demodulates AM: the air band (118 to 137 MHz), and the short and medium
wave broadcasts through direct sampling or an upconverter.
The samples are summed to about 240k a second, the channel
.RB ( -b
hertz either side, 5000) is filtered out and they are decimated to
about 48k.
Each one's magnitude over the carrier's (its magnitude averaged over a
tenth of a second) is the sound, so the level out does not follow the
signal's strength; full modulation is 0.7 of full scale.
.PP
Both take:
.TP
.BI -o " rate"
the rate out: 8000 by default; higher for broadcast FM
.RI ( audio (3)
takes 8000, 11025, 22050 and 44100).
.TP
.B -R
raw sound, no header.
.TP
.BI -c " chans"
the channels out (2, as
.IR audio (3)).
.TP
.BI -g " gain"
the volume multiplied; past full scale is clipped.
.PP
A rate out the rate in cannot give is refused.
.PP
.I Rawiq
gives samples with no header, such as a capture by
.IR rtl_sdr ,
the header
.I fm
and
.I am
need: it is the single place a capture's rate is said, as
.IR raw2iaf (1)
is for sound.
.B -r
is the rate and
.BR -f ,
if given, the frequency, each with k, M or G taken.
.PP
.I Iqhdr
writes the header of samples on its standard input as commands, one a
line, in one write, so that
.B sdr/iqhdr < rec > /mnt/sdr/ctl
tunes the radio to what a recording
.RB ( "cat /mnt/sdr/iq > rec" ,
which keeps the header) was made at, all of it or, if
.B ctl
refuses a line, none.
.SH EXAMPLES
A broadcast station, to the speaker:
.IP
.EX
; sdrfs source localhost
; echo 'freq 98.5M
rate 2.4M
gain 20' > /mnt/sdr/ctl
; sdr/fm -o 44100 < /mnt/sdr/iq | auplay /fd/0
.EE
.PP
The air band's emergency frequency:
.IP
.EX
; echo freq 121.5M > /mnt/sdr/ctl
; sdr/am -o 22050 < /mnt/sdr/iq | auplay /fd/0
.EE
.PP
A recording made once, demodulated later, and the radio put back where
it was made:
.IP
.EX
; cat /mnt/sdr/iq > /tmp/marine.iq
; sdr/fm -n -c 1 < /tmp/marine.iq | auplay /fd/0
; sdr/iqhdr < /tmp/marine.iq > /mnt/sdr/ctl
.EE
.PP
A capture with no header, by
.IR rtl_sdr ,
on another machine:
.IP
.EX
; sdr/rawiq -r 2.4M -f 99.5M < capture.cu8 | sdr/fm -o 44100 | auplay /fd/0
.EE
